### What makes a system genuinely complex?

Complexity in a banking system at National Bank of India comes from a few well-known sources. Scale: millions of transactions a day stress throughput and storage. Concurrency: many transfers touching Rajesh's and Priya's accounts at once create race conditions. Distributed state: when the ledger, the fraud service, and the notification service are separate, keeping them consistent is hard. Strict correctness: money must balance exactly. Many integrations: partner banks, payment networks, and regulators each add surface area.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

import java.util.List;

public class ComplexitySources {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> sources = List.of(
            "Scale: millions of txns/day",
            "Concurrency: same account hit by parallel transfers",
            "Distributed state: ledger + fraud + notification are separate",
            "Strict correctness: every rupee must balance",
            "Many integrations: partner banks, payment networks, regulators"
        );
        sources.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

### What is the difference between essential and accidental complexity?

Essential complexity is inherent to the problem : settlement of millions of transactions with strict correctness is genuinely hard. Accidental complexity is what your design adds unnecessarily : a tangled dependency graph, inconsistent naming, or a shared database schema that couples unrelated services. The senior move is to attack the accidental kind.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class ComplexityTypes {
    enum Type { ESSENTIAL, ACCIDENTAL }

    public static Type classify(String source) {
        return switch (source) {
            case "ACID transactions for money"   -> Type.ESSENTIAL;
            case "Tangled service dependencies"   -> Type.ACCIDENTAL;
            case "Concurrent account access"      -> Type.ESSENTIAL;
            case "Shared database coupling"       -> Type.ACCIDENTAL;
            default -> Type.ESSENTIAL;
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("ACID: " + classify("ACID transactions for money"));
        System.out.println("Coupling: " + classify("Shared database coupling"));
    }
}
```

```text
ACID: ESSENTIAL
Coupling: ACCIDENTAL
```

### How does decomposition reduce complexity?

Each service owns exactly one responsibility: Settlement settles, Fraud scores, Notification notifies. Each piece stays simple even though the whole system is large.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class ServiceDecomposition {
    interface SettlementService { void settle(String transferId); }
    interface FraudService      { double score(String transferId); }
    interface NotificationService { void notify(String customerId, String message); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Settlement: owns settling transfers");
        System.out.println("Fraud: owns risk scoring");
        System.out.println("Notification: owns customer alerts");
        System.out.println("Each is simple. The whole is manageable.");
    }
}
```

### How does idempotency make retries safe?

In a distributed system, retries are inevitable. Keying each transfer by a request id means a repeat can never double-debit Rajesh's account.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class IdempotentLedger {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> processed = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public String apply(String requestId, String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount) {
        String existing = processed.get(requestId);
        if (existing != null) {
            return existing; // safe retry: return same result
        }
        String result = doTransfer(fromAccount, toAccount, amount);
        processed.put(requestId, result);
        return result;
    }

    private String doTransfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        return "SETTLED: " + from + " -> " + to + " = " + amount;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        IdempotentLedger ledger = new IdempotentLedger();
        String r1 = ledger.apply("REQ-001", "SAV2001", "SAV2002", new BigDecimal("5000.00"));
        String r2 = ledger.apply("REQ-001", "SAV2001", "SAV2002", new BigDecimal("5000.00"));
        System.out.println("First:  " + r1);
        System.out.println("Retry:  " + r2);
        System.out.println("Same?   " + r1.equals(r2));
    }
}
```

```text
First:  SETTLED: SAV2001 -> SAV2002 = 5000.00
Retry:  SETTLED: SAV2001 -> SAV2002 = 5000.00
Same?   true
```

### How does observability help manage what you cannot hold in your head?

A correlation id follows one transaction through every service, so you can trace Priya's transfer end to end even when the system is too large for any one person to understand all of it.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.UUID;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class CorrelationTrace {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(CorrelationTrace.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String correlationId = UUID.randomUUID().toString().substring(0, 8);
        log.info(() -> "[" + correlationId + "] Transfer requested: SAV2001 -> SAV2002");
        log.info(() -> "[" + correlationId + "] Fraud score: 0.12 (safe)");
        log.info(() -> "[" + correlationId + "] Settlement complete");
        log.info(() -> "[" + correlationId + "] Notification sent to Priya");
    }
}
```

### How do you treat failure as a first-class concern?

In a distributed system, parts will fail. You design for it with retries, timeouts, circuit breakers, and reconciliation jobs that detect and repair inconsistencies.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class CircuitBreakerExample {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(CircuitBreakerExample.class.getName());
    private boolean open = false;
    private int failureCount = 0;
    private static final int THRESHOLD = 3;

    public String callDownstream(String transferId) {
        if (open) {
            log.warning("Circuit OPEN: returning fallback for " + transferId);
            return "FALLBACK";
        }
        try {
            return doCall(transferId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            failureCount++;
            if (failureCount >= THRESHOLD) { open = true; }
            return "FALLBACK";
        }
    }

    private String doCall(String transferId) { return "OK"; }
}
```

### How do you design for safe evolution over years?

A complex system must be changed safely over years. Backward-compatible contracts mean a new version of Settlement can be deployed without breaking Notification. Feature flags mean a new code path can be tested on one percent of traffic before full rollout.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FeatureFlagEvolution {
    private static final boolean NEW_SETTLEMENT_ENABLED = false;

    public static String settle(String transferId) {
        if (NEW_SETTLEMENT_ENABLED) {
            return newSettlementPath(transferId);
        }
        return legacySettlementPath(transferId);
    }

    private static String newSettlementPath(String id) { return "NEW:" + id; }
    private static String legacySettlementPath(String id) { return "LEGACY:" + id; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(settle("TXN-5001"));
    }
}
```

```text
LEGACY:TXN-5001
```

### What should a senior engineer emphasize about cognitive load?

Good boundaries and naming let a human reason about one piece without understanding all of it. The goal is not cleverness but simplicity at each layer.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class CognitiveLoad {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Principle: each service should be understandable in isolation.");
        System.out.println("Settlement knows nothing about Notification internals.");
        System.out.println("Good naming + clear contracts = low cognitive load.");
    }
}
```
